==English==

===Etymology===
Recorded since 1647, from {{etyl|ML.|en}} {{term|culminatus|lang=la}}, the past participle of {{term|culminare||to crown|lang=la}}, from {{etyl|la|en}} {{term|culmen||peak, the highest point|lang=la}}, older form {{term|columen||top, summit|lang=la}}, from a Proto-Indo-European base *kel- "to project".

===Pronunciation===
* {{audio|en-uk-culminate.ogg|Audio (UK)|lang=en}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb|culminat}}

# {{context|intransitive|astronomy|lang=en}} Of a heavenly body, to be at the highest point, reach its greatest altitude.
# {{context|intransitive|lang=en}} To [[reach]] the (physical) [[summit]], highest point, [[peak]] etc.
#* Milton
#*: As when his beams at noon / '''Culminate''' from the equator.
#* Dana
#*: The reptile race '''culminated''' in the secondary era.
#* Motley
#*: The house of Burgundy was rapidly '''culminating'''.
# {{context|intransitive|lang=en}} To reach a [[climax]]; to come to the decisive point (especially as an end or conclusion).
#: ''Their messy breakup '''culminated''' in a restraining order.''
#: [http://www.nytimes.com/2006/09/12/opinion/12tue1.html?ex=1158292800&en=baef3598e5784194&ei=5087%0A New York Times] ''Mr. Bush has been marking the fifth anniversary of Sept. 11 with a series of speeches about terrorism that '''culminated''' with his televised address last night.''
#: ''The class will '''culminate''' with a rigorous examination.''
# {{context|transitive|lang=en}} To [[finalize]], bring to a [[conclusion]], form the climax of.
#* '''2010''', "By the skin of her teeth", ''The Economist'', 7 Sep 2010:
#*: The announcement by Tony Windsor and Rob Oakeshott in Canberra '''culminated''' more than a fortnight of intensive political horse-trading.

